  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Piloting a Constructed Wetland System for Wastewater Treatment in Southern Iraq</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Iraq has been named the fifth most vulnerable country to adverse effects of climate change affected by soaring temperatures, insufficient and diminishing rainfall, intensified droughts and water scarcity, frequent sand and dust storms, and reoccurring floodings. In addition to this, water policies in neighbouring countries have shrunk vital water sources in the region, while rapid population growth, urbanization, and inefficient water use by the agricultural and industrial sectors is propelling an increasing demand for water. The scale of environmental change is likely to be devastating and may force Iraqis to relocate in order to survive. Through this pilot project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is supporting marshland and rural communities in southern Iraq in coping with the complex crisis of drought, water pollution, and climate change using nature-based solutions. This is being achieved through improving water quality and enhancing ecosystem services to sustain livelihoods dependent on affected wetlands.</narrative>
  </description>
